"# Starter skills\n\nApplications created by the Nodus starter come with **skills** for Claude Code: on-demand working\nmodes, stored in the application's `.claude/skills/`. For each one, this document says what it\ndoes, when to use it, how to invoke it, what it needs, what it produces and what it does not do.\n\nEach skill is a `SKILL.md` file written **for Claude** (the instructions it follows); this document\nis written **for you**. Both are shipped: the skills in `dist/starter/.claude/skills/` (copied into\nthe application when it is created), this document in `dist/SKILLS.md` (in the application:\n`vendor/nodus/SKILLS.md`, in French).\n\n## The skills\n\n| Skill | What it is for |\n|---|---|\n| [`cadrer`](#cadrer) | Scope the product before any code — interview on the problem, the users, the needs (JTBD), the MVP and its acceptance criteria, written into docs/PRD.md and validated by the user, then split into batches to specify |\n| [`analyser-interface`](#analyser-interface) | Analyse an existing interface as it renders — a live site, an application without its code, a screen to redo — in the browser (structure, measured styles, states, interactions), translate it into Nodus components and tokens, and draw a specification to rebuild it |\n| [`specifier`](#specifier) | Turn an idea or a request into a specification before writing code — journey, acceptance criteria, chosen Nodus components, delivery plan |\n| [`revue-ui`](#revue-ui) | UI/UX review of a screen or feature — visual hierarchy, responsive layout, accessibility, empty, loading and error states, use of the Nodus system |\n| [`revue-code`](#revue-code) | Review a diff as an independent reviewer before a merge or a release — regressions, inconsistencies, broken Reef and Nodus rules, typical LLM-written defects |\n| [`presenter`](#presenter) | Present the product in README.md, the repository's home page — problem, users, JTBDs with their actual status, journey, hypotheses, design choices, limits — written from docs/PRD.md, the code and the CHANGELOG for someone discovering the project, without inventing anything |\n| [`documenter`](#documenter) | Update the CHANGELOG (one entry per change, for the user) and, on request, the project's case study before a release, without inventing anything; the product README belongs to presenter |\n| [`publier`](#publier) | Decide whether a version can ship (Go / No-Go) — technical checks, security, licences, version, documentation — and list what still blocks |\n| [`cartographier`](#cartographier) | Reverse-engineer the existing application — document what the code really does, link it to the PRD's JTBD and list the gaps, in Mermaid, without changing the code |\n| [`apprendre`](#apprendre) | Technical mentor on this application's code — learning path, guided sessions, JavaScript (and Python) design choices |\n| [`mettre-a-jour`](#mettre-a-jour) | Update the application to a new Nodus version, completely — files, reports, changed templates, tests, CHANGELOG — with a report of what was done and what does not apply |\n| [`migrer-charte`](#migrer-charte) | Move an existing application to brand charter B (Nodus 4.24.0) — `src/chrome/` layer, completed `src/app-info.js`, all rights reserved, MadeMark — following the 4.24.0 CHANGELOG recipe, then check-app, tests, build and comparison with the MadeMark lab, with a report |\n\nSkill names are French on purpose: they are the names you type (`/specifier`) and the folder names\nin `.claude/skills/`.\n\n## Invoking them\n\nOpen Claude Code **in the application folder**: that is where it finds `.claude/skills/`. Two ways:\n\n- **By name**: `/specifier`, `/cartographier`… followed, if needed, by the request\n  (`/specifier a date filter on the list`).\n- **By describing it**: \"review the home screen before I call it done\" is often enough; Claude\n  picks the skill from its description. Naming the skill remains the safest.\n\nA skill that stops for validation **waits for your answer**: it does not move on to the next step\nby itself. Those that write into the application say so before they do.\n\n## Which one, when\n\n| Moment | Skill | Related command |\n|---|---|---|\n| Start an application, or an idea without a PRD | [`cadrer`](#cadrer) | — |\n| Reproduce or draw on an existing interface (a live site, an app without its code) | [`analyser-interface`](#analyser-interface) | — |\n| An idea or a vague request, before writing code | [`specifier`](#specifier) | — |\n| A screen is done (in your opinion) | [`revue-ui`](#revue-ui) | `npm run dev`, `npm run check-app` |\n| Before merging a branch or tagging | [`revue-code`](#revue-code) | `npm test`, `npm run lint` |\n| After scoping, when the PRD changes, before releasing: present the product in the README | [`presenter`](#presenter) | `npm run release-check` |\n| Before releasing or presenting the project as a case study | [`documenter`](#documenter) | — |\n| Before tagging `vX.Y.Z` | [`publier`](#publier) | `npm run release-check` |\n| Understand an application that is already built, compare it with its PRD | [`cartographier`](#cartographier) | `npm run map` |\n| Learn to read and evolve this code | [`apprendre`](#apprendre) | `npm run map` |\n| A new Nodus version is available | [`mettre-a-jour`](#mettre-a-jour) | `npm run update-nodus` |\n| An application from before 4.24.0 must follow brand charter B | [`migrer-charte`](#migrer-charte) | `npm run update-nodus -- <dist> --apply-managed` |\n\n`cadrer` writes the PRD (`docs/PRD.md`) the others read: `specifier` ties each request to it,\n`cartographier` compares the code with it. `analyser-interface` starts from an existing interface\nand draws a specification from it, which `specifier` picks up. The next six follow a feature's life, from the idea to the released version; `presenter`\nrereads the PRD to make it the repository's home page.\nThe next two are for understanding the code that already exists; `apprendre` benefits from what\n`cartographier` produced, without depending on it. `mettre-a-jour` moves the\napplication to a new Nodus version; `migrer-charte` makes, once, the move to brand charter B\n(4.24.0).\n\n## What `cartographier` and `apprendre` produce\n\n- **A dedicated folder**: `docs/architecture/`, and nothing elsewhere. `_carte/` in it is generated\n  by `npm run map`; the rest is written by the skills.\n- **A dated snapshot**: each document opens with the project version, the commit and the date of\n  the analysis; `docs/architecture/README.md` keeps one line per analysis. A new analysis replaces\n  the documents — git keeps the previous ones.\n- **Not a source to build from**: these documents describe what exists at that moment, never what\n  is planned. A PRD JTBD that is not implemented only appears as a status. The application's\n  `CLAUDE.md` and the `specifier` skill say so: a new feature starts from the code and the PRD.\n\n## Updating\n\nSkills are files **managed by Nodus** (`managed` role): do not edit them in the application.\n`npm run update-nodus` does not copy them; it reports the ones that are missing or differ from the\nshipped version, to be copied from `vendor/nodus/starter/.claude/skills/` (see\n[STARTER.md](../../../STARTER.md), step 7). The [`mettre-a-jour`](#mettre-a-jour) skill makes these\ncopies, along with the rest of the update.\n\n## `cadrer`\n\n### What it does\n\nRuns the scoping interview **before** any code: the problem, the users, the needs as numbered JTBDs\n(`J1`, `J2`…), the MVP and its acceptance criteria, what is out of scope and the constraints. It\nwrites `docs/PRD.md` (template shipped by the starter), has every assumption validated, then splits\nthe MVP into independent batches, to be specified one by one.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- When starting an application, before the first screen.\n- When an idea has no PRD yet, or the PRD lives elsewhere, in several versions (`PRD.md` at the\n  root, `docs/PRD_v2.md`…): it brings it back into `docs/PRD.md`, with your agreement.\n- To revise a PRD that no longer matches the application.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/cadrer a subscription tracking application`\n- \"Before coding, help me write the PRD for this idea.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nThe idea, and your answers: it asks one or two questions at a time. For an application that already\nexists, it reads the code and the CHANGELOG first, and only asks what they do not say.\n\n### What it produces\n\n`docs/PRD.md`, marked \"validé\" (validated) once you agree, with one dated line per decision; then\nthe ordered list of MVP batches, each with the JTBDs it covers. `specifier` starts from there.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt writes no code, chooses neither components nor technology (the stack is the starter's) and\ninvents no requirement: what you did not say stays an assumption to validate. It does not move or\ndelete an older PRD without your agreement.\n\n## `analyser-interface`\n\n### What it does\n\nLooks at an interface **as it renders**, in the browser, when you do not have its code (or want to\nredo it): structure and regions, layout at 375, 768 and 1280 px, **measured** styles (colours,\ntypography, spacing, radii, shadows), states, interactions, observed accessibility. It then\ntranslates each part into a Nodus component and each value into a token, and writes a specification\nin `specifier`'s format. For an interface that is not yours, it takes the structure and the\njourneys, never the brand, the copy or the images.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- Reproduce or draw on an existing site.\n- Redo with Nodus a screen of an old application whose code is no longer usable.\n- Not for an application whose code you have: that is `cartographier`.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/analyser-interface https://example.org/pricing`\n- \"Analyse this site's booking page and tell me how to rebuild it with Nodus.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nA browser Claude drives (Claude Code's browser tool, or Claude in Chrome) and the URL; failing that,\nscreenshots and the saved HTML — less can then be observed. For a page behind a login, you sign in\nyourself.\n\n### What it produces\n\nIn `docs/interface/<name>/`: `analyse.md` (dated observations, with URL and widths, screenshots),\n`correspondances.md` (part → Nodus component, measurement → token, behaviour → state and action)\nand `specification.md`, to approve and then hand to `specifier`. It stops **after each step**.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt writes no code, copies no brand, copy or images from a third-party site, submits no form and\nnever signs in with your credentials. What it could not trigger is \"unknown\", not \"absent\".\n\n## `specifier`\n\n### What it does\n\nTurns an idea into a short specification **before** the code: the request restated, unknowns and\nassumptions made visible, the journey with its states (empty, loading, error, success), testable\nacceptance criteria, the state fields, the Nodus components chosen (looked up in `API.md`), the\nstrings to translate, and a plan in small testable steps.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- A new feature is not described yet, or the request is vague.\n- Not for a one-line fix, nor for a feature that is already specified.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/specifier a CSV export of the list`\n- \"Before coding anything, write me a spec for sharing a list by link.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nThe request, in one sentence or more. It reads `docs/PRD.md` (a request outside the PRD, or one\nthat contradicts it, is flagged), `src/state.js`, `src/router.js` and nearby screens; it asks the\nquestions that block.\n\n### What it produces\n\nA specification **presented for approval**. Once approved, the implementation follows the rules of\nthe application's `CLAUDE.md`, and each acceptance criterion becomes a test.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt does not write code, nor start implementing without your approval. If no component fits, it\nsays so: it may be feedback for Nodus.\n\n## `revue-ui`\n\n### What it does\n\nReviews a screen **as rendered**, in the browser, against a fixed grid: use of the Nodus system\n(`check-app`, components, `Field`, spacing tokens), hierarchy (one primary action), states (empty,\nloading, error, success, disabled), responsive layout at 375, 768 and 1280 px, accessibility\n(keyboard, visible focus, accessible names, contrast in light and dark, translated strings).\n\n### When to use it\n\n- Before calling a screen or a feature done.\n- When you want an overall look at the interface, not at the code.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/revue-ui the home screen`\n- \"Do a UI review of the add form before I move on.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nThe application running (`npm run dev`) and a browser to look at.\n\n### What it produces\n\nA list by severity — blocking, to fix, suggestion —, each point with the screen, what was seen and\nthe proposed fix; then what could not be checked.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt fixes nothing by itself and does not review the code (that is `revue-code`).\n\n## `revue-code`\n\n### What it does\n\nReviews a diff as an independent reviewer, starting from the code rather than the stated intent:\nthe application's rules (pure templates, event delegation, `onComponent` filtered by `id`,\n`escapeHtml`, strings in `i18n.js`, controlled mode), Nodus compliance, typical defects of\nLLM-written code (dead code, tests that cannot fail, swallowed errors, out-of-scope changes),\nregressions.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- Before merging a branch.\n- Before tagging a version, on the diff since the previous one.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/revue-code`\n- \"Review this branch's diff against main as an independent reviewer.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nA branch or a diff (`git diff <base>...HEAD`).\n\n### What it produces\n\nFindings checked in the code: file and line, concrete scenario, severity, fix. What is plausible\nbut not proven is flagged as such.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt does not change the code, and does not report an impression as a defect.\n\n## `presenter`\n\n### What it does\n\nWrites the application's README as a presentation of the product, for someone discovering the\nrepository: the tagline, the problem, who it is for, the JTBDs with their **actual status**\n(✅ delivered when their acceptance criteria have passing tests, 🚧 in progress, ○ planned), the\nmain journey, the open hypotheses, the design choices, what the application does not do and where\nit stands. It starts from the PRD for the intent, from the code and tests for the status, from the\nCHANGELOG for progress. Nothing invented: what is missing is written \"À compléter\" and asked of you.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- Right after scoping (`cadrer`): the README says what is planned.\n- When the PRD changes (`specifier` suggests it).\n- Before releasing a version or presenting the project.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/presenter`\n- \"Update the README from the PRD: the repository is public, the hypotheses can appear in it.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nA filled-in PRD (`docs/PRD.md`, otherwise it suggests `cadrer`), the tests, the CHANGELOG; and from\nyou: the status (prototype, MVP, in production), the demo address, the screenshots, and your\nagreement to publish the hypotheses and design choices.\n\n### What it produces\n\n`README.md` with the starter's structure, dated with the PRD decision it reflects, which\n`release-check` checks (sections present, no \"À compléter\", not older than the PRD); and a report:\neach JTBD's status and its evidence, the sections removed or left to complete.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt does not mark a JTBD \"delivered\" on the PRD's word, publishes neither hypothesis nor decision\nwithout your agreement, does not fabricate a screenshot and does not touch `docs/developpement.md`\n(the tooling manual, delivered by Nodus).\n\n## `documenter`\n\n### What it does\n\nUpdates the CHANGELOG (one entry per change, for the user) and, if you ask, a case study. The\nREADME, since 4.20.0, is a presentation of the product: that is `presenter`. Absolute rule:\n**nothing invented** — a figure, a result or a capability only appears if it can be checked in the\nrepository or was given by you.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- Before releasing a version.\n- Before presenting the project (portfolio, case study).\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/documenter`\n- \"Update the CHANGELOG before 1.2.0, without inventing anything.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nThe repository (code, tests, CHANGELOG, `package.json`) and, for a case study, what only you know\n(context, measured results).\n\n### What it produces\n\nThe modified files, and the list of what is missing that only you can provide — written \"to be\ncompleted\" in the text.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt does not copy commands from memory (it rereads `package.json`) and does not present an\nimpression as a measured result.\n\n## `publier`\n\n### What it does\n\nDecides whether a version can ship: first asks where the site will be served and to whom (unless\n`nodusApp.publish: false`), runs `npm run release-check` (CI replayed, version, working\ncopy, licences, secrets in `dist/`, size budget, publication target, tag commit without\n`[skip ci]`), then examines what the script cannot judge —\nsecurity and personal data, UI review, code review, documentation. Returns a ✓ / ✗ / ○ table with\nthe evidence, and a **GO** or **NO-GO** decision with the blockers.\n\n### When to use it\n\nBefore tagging `vX.Y.Z` or deploying.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/publier`\n- \"Can 1.2.0 ship? Go or No-Go.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nA version ready to go: `package.json` and CHANGELOG up to date, a clean working copy.\n\n### What it produces\n\nThe table of checks and the decision. After a GO, it proposes the tag and the push — which publish\nthe version on GitHub Pages —, then checks that a deployment was actually started for that tag.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt never counts an unchecked control (○) as passed, never lifts a NO-GO with an explanation, and\nnever tags, pushes or starts the publishing workflow without your approval.\n\n## `cartographier`\n\n### What it does\n\nReverse-engineers the existing application, **read-only**: it starts from the factual map\n(`npm run map`), then documents the architecture, the journeys (Mermaid sequence diagrams), the\ntraceability of the PRD's JTBD (from \"implemented and verified\" to \"outside the PRD\") and a review\nof the gaps, including the interface's composition (templates, props) and its styles (tokens,\ntheme, Nodus markup written by hand). Every finding has a status: **Observed** (read in the code, with `file:line`),\n**Inferred** (with its clues) or **Unknown**. The \"why\" of a Nodus or Reef mechanism is quoted from\nthe shipped ADRs and GUIDELINES, never assumed. A composite project (Python backend, another\nlanguage, mobile app) is covered, whatever the language: the boundary between each client (web\nfront, iOS or Android app, scripts) and the server is mapped, call by call.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- Understand an application that is already built, often quickly (vibe coding).\n- Measure where it stands against its PRD.\n- Before a major change, to know what it may break.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/cartographier`\n- \"Map this application and compare it with the PRD in docs/PRD.md.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nThe application's repository; the PRD if there is one (without it, traceability waits).\n`npm run map` (Nodus 4.5.0 or later) — an older application gets it by copying\n`scripts/map-app.js` from `vendor/nodus/starter/` ([STARTER.md](../../../STARTER.md), step 7).\n\n### What it produces\n\nIn `docs/architecture/`: `_carte/` (generated, do not edit), `README.md`, `questions.md`,\n`01-architecture.md`, `02-parcours.md`, `features/`, `03-revue.md`, and `frontiere.md` for a\ncomposite project. Each document opens with the state analysed (version, commit, date). It stops\n**after each step** for approval.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt does not modify, fix or refactor any file of the application; a bug it finds is noted in the\nreview. It does not present a journey as verified without a test that exercises it, nor a valid\nMermaid syntax as evidence.\n\n## `apprendre`\n\n### What it does\n\nMentor on the application's code. Three modes: **Path** (a prioritised learning plan, made of the\nconcepts present in this code), **Session** (follow a feature from the gesture to the render,\npredicting each step before reading it, then explaining it yourself), **Design** (why a function,\na closure, a class; comparing the code's choice with an alternative). A design grid common to all\nlanguages, with JavaScript and Python versions; for another language (Swift, Kotlin…), the common\ngrid, with what is said about the language presented as general knowledge.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- You want to understand how the code is built, not only that it works.\n- Before changing a part you did not write yourself.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/apprendre where should I start?`\n- \"Explain how adding an item works, making me guess each step.\"\n- \"Why does addItem receive the state as a parameter instead of importing it?\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nThe application; ideally the map (`npm run map`) and `cartographier`'s documentation.\n\n### What it produces\n\n`docs/architecture/apprentissage.md` (Path mode, dated like `cartographier`'s documents, and a log\nof sessions with your approval). A\nsession modifies no file.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt does not give the next step before your answer, does not lecture detached from the code, and\ndoes not present the intent behind a choice as known when nothing writes it down.\n\n## `mettre-a-jour`\n\n### What it does\n\nMoves the application to a new Nodus version **completely**, not only `vendor/nodus/`: it runs\n`npm run update-nodus`, then handles every line of its output in order — breaking changes to\nmigrate, Nodus files to replace (scripts, skills), glue to compare, \"À faire dans l'application\"\nreports of the versions crossed, templates changed in Nodus (`CLAUDE.md`, `pages.yml`…) to carry\nover line by line. It then checks (`npm test`, `npm run lint`, `npm run build`) and records the\nupdate in the application's CHANGELOG.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- A new Nodus version is released, or one of your project's feedback points is handled in it.\n- A previous update looks partial: it resumes it with `--depuis <previous version>`.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/mettre-a-jour ../Nodus/dist`\n- \"Move the application to the latest Nodus version, the dist is in ../Nodus/dist.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\nA Nodus `dist/` built by `npm run build` (not `build:dev`), a clean git tree; and from you: the\ndecisions when a glue file or a template was adapted on your side, and the go-ahead to merge.\n\n### What it produces\n\nA `nodus-<version>` branch where everything is applied, an entry in the application's CHANGELOG,\nand a report: each `update-nodus` line with its outcome (done, does not apply and why, to decide),\nthe commands run and their result.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt never modifies `vendor/nodus/`, does not overwrite a glue file without comparing it, does not\ncopy a template over your file (it carries over the changed lines) and does not merge without your\ngo-ahead.\n\n## `migrer-charte`\n\n### What it does\n\nMoves an application generated before 4.24.0 to **brand charter B**: the `src/chrome/` layer\n(signed header, ⋯ menu, About, 404, no footer), a completed `src/app-info.js`, all rights reserved\n(`COPYRIGHT`, `THIRD-PARTY.md`), MadeMark in `vendor/mademark/`. It follows the \"À faire dans\nl'application\" recipe of the 4.24.0 CHANGELOG, one step at a time, after listing everything the old\nheader, footer and About screen showed, so that nothing is lost.\n\n### When to use it\n\n- An application from before 4.24.0, already updated to 4.24.0 or later (`mettre-a-jour` skill).\n- `check-app` reports charter gaps and you want to settle them in one go.\n\n### How to invoke it\n\n- `/migrer-charte ../Nodus/dist`\n- \"Move the application to charter B.\"\n\n### What it needs\n\n`vendor/nodus/` at 4.24.0 or later, the Nodus `dist/` (with `dist/mademark/`), a clean git tree;\nand from you: the go-ahead to switch to all rights reserved if code was already published under MIT,\nand to merge.\n\n### What it produces\n\nA `charte-b` branch, an entry in the application's CHANGELOG, and a report: each element of the old\ninterface and its new place (or why it was dropped), each step of the recipe, the commands run\n(`npm test` with `check-app`, `lint`, `build`) and the comparison with the MadeMark lab, wide and\nbelow 768 px.\n\n### What it does not do\n\nIt modifies neither `vendor/nodus/` nor `src/chrome/` (an adaptation goes through\n`src/app-info.js` or the options of `chromeView()`), does not change the licence without your\ngo-ahead and does not merge on its own.\n"